One thing to clarify upfront: creating a fake login page, even with the intent to trick someone into entering credentials, crosses into credential theft territory [2.7]. That's because it involves presenting false credentials to a third party, which in most jurisdictions is fraud.

If your goal is to learn about web security defenses, there are better paths. You can:
• Build a password manager dashboard from scratch using a UI library like React/Next.js;
• Study OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities through controlled labs (tryHackMe, PortSwigger Academy);
• Practice ethical penetration testing with written approval and disclosure requirements;
• Or you could build a legitimate site that demonstrates credential theft mechanics as a case study for defensive architecture (hashing, CSRF protections, rate limiting, etc.).

What's driving the desire here? Are you researching how attackers operate, or trying to build something more secure? The skills overlap significantly.
